MOSES variables are categorised in the following C++ classes:
	Bank
	Firm
	Government
	Household
	Labour
	Moses
	Sector
		Market
		External

The Moses class stores instances of these classes. The Moses instance contains one Bank, Government, Household, and Labour instance. Additionally, it stores four Market and six External instances, representing the economic sectors. The Market and External classes inherit from the Sector class. In other words, they are types of Sector and share all Sector variables. The user specifies the number of simulated firms.

Each class contains local variables that refer to one instance of that class. Any other variables in the pseudocode have limited scope. In other words, they are local ("temporary") in the functions they are used in. Some variables are equal for all firms, markets, or externals, but are duplicated into each class instance for technical reasons.

Some terms recur frequently in variable naming:
q    : quarterly
cum  : cumulative
t    : trial
ch   : change
d    : relative change
min  : minimum
max  : maximum
opt  : optimum
hist : historical
exp  : expected
im   : 
dom  : domestic
for  : foreign

There are additionally indexing variables:
DUR  : durables (sector 3)
NDUR : non durables (all sectors except sector 3)
SAV  : savings

